# Patterning Techniques for Metal Organic Frameworks

## abstract
The tuneable pore size and architecture, chemical properties and functionalization make metal organic frameworks (MOFs) attractive versatile stimuli-responsive materials. In this context, MOFs hold promise for industrial applications and a fervent research field is currently investigating MOF properties for device fabrication. Although the material properties have a crucial role, the ability to precisely locate the functional material is fundamental for device fabrication. In this progress report, advancements in the control of MOF positioning and precise localization of functional materials within MOF crystals are presented. Advantages and limitations of each reviewed technique are critically investigated, and several important gaps in the technological development for device fabrication are highlighted. Finally, promising patterning techniques are presented which are inspired by previous studies in organic and inorganic crystal patterning for the future of MOF lithography.

The tuneable pore size and architecture, chemical properties and functionalization make metal organic frameworks (MOFs) attractive versatile stimuliresponsive materials. In this context, MOFs hold promise for industrial applications and a fervent research field is currently investigating MOF properties for device fabrication. Although the material properties have a crucial role, the ability to precisely locate the functional material is fundamental for device fabrication. In this progress report, advancements in the control of MOF positioning and precise localization of functional materials within MOF crystals are presented. Advantages and limitations of each reviewed technique are critically investigated, and several important gaps in the technological development for device fabrication are highlighted. Finally, promising patterning techniques are presented which are inspired by previous studies in organic and inorganic crystal patterning for the future of MOF lithography.
## 1. Introduction
Fabrication of functional materials into appropriate devices will advance the development of important technologies. Demand for new technologies arises from the need to rapidly process data, 1 2  to efficiently produce and store energy, 3  to sense hazardous contaminants 4  and to improve human health through the early detection of deadly diseases. 5–7  Advances in both new materials and innovative fabrication procedures will lead to progress in numerous fields such as these and will enable the preparation of novel integrated platforms. 6 8 9  Recent innovations in micro- and nano-fabrication have created a unique opportunity to construct structures ranging in size from nanometers to several hundred microns. 10  Integrated circuit manufacturing and related industries such as microsystems, solar cells, flat-panel displays, optoelectronics, ink-jet printing and an increasing number of analytical chemistry devices already rely on microfabrication technologies. 11–13  Highly integrated devices on the micron-scale have shown great promise as sensors in biomedical and pharmaceutical research. Indeed, integrated microfluidic devices can perform rapid and reproducible measurements on reduced sample volumes while eliminating the need for labour-intensive and potentially error-prone laboratory operations. 14 15  In addition, versatile integrated microfluidic devices developed for parallel cell culture and combinatorial biological assays in a quasi-automated or automated fashion are predicted to revolutionize microbiology and medical screening. 16–18 Information technology is another field that has benefited from breakthrough advances in combining positioning/ scaling capabilities (micro- and nanofabrication techniques) with the precise control of electrical material properties allowing the production of devices with improved performance. 19–21
In a broader context, where precise control of locating materials with exceptional features potentially offers an advantage, new materials are continuously being integrated into miniaturized devices. 22  The discovery of novel fabrication protocols to enhance phenomena occurring at the small scale 11 23 and the improvement of existing microfabrication techniques (increasing resolution, making the process easier, faster, cheaper or more versatile) 24  are both crucial aspects of this technological progress. Despite the availability of protocols for the synthesis of advanced materials with outstanding properties, their integration into miniaturized devices is often a difficult task. In the last two decades, porous materials have attracted enormous technological interest for a variety of applications; 25  however, the ability to position porous materials has been broadly investigated only for a specific class of high surface area materials (e.g. mesoporous coatings 26 ); for other porous materials the research is in its infancy.
Methods to precisely control the location of porous materials are now being applied to matrices with exceptional properties such as metal organic frameworks (MOFs), also called coordination polymers. MOFs are an emerging class of ultra-porous hybrid materials which originate through the self-assembly of polydentate bridging ligands (usually carboxylate, sulfonate, or phosphonate) resulting in the formation of well-defined networks with high porosity. 27–31  MOFs are extensively studied as they are very promising for a variety of applications due to their large surface area, tuneable pore size, chemistry and topology which leads to versatile architectures. 32
Precise control of the framework features offers the opportunity to prepare materials responsive to a variety of external stimuli 33  (e.g. photoswitchable MOFs have been prepared using photo-sensitive linkers). 34  In addition, MOFs can be synergistically combined with a variety of functional heterogeneous species in order to prepare novel multifunctional materials. 35–38  MOFs were initially investigated for their outstanding gas storage properties 39  and more recently studied for separation, 30  micro- and nano-electronics, 40 41  sensing 42  as well as biological and medical applications. 28 43
A number of reviews have been recently published in order to give comprehensive overviews of different aspects related to MOFs, such as their synthesis, 44–46  post-synthetic modification techniques, 47  concepts/properties 48 49  and possible applications. 50 51  Synthetic routes for MOF preparation are crucial to achieving their great potential for tuneable electric, optical and mechanical properties, as summarized by Allendorf et al. 52 40  The development of new efficient tools or protocols to precisely control the MOF position on substrates would offer enormous advantages for their technological application in electronics, sensing, storage, separation, and catalysis which require ultraporous materials within a miniaturized device.
The first step towards producing MOF based devices was the development of MOF film formation which remains a fervent research activity. Different research groups have made significant progress, pioneering such an important field; in particular Fisher, 53 54  Kitagawa 55  and the Sanchez and Férey 56–58  groups have presented innovative methodologies to prepare MOF based films using different strategies. However, the incorporation of these self-assembled structures into a device is a complex task 59  and there are only a small number of cases reported to date where MOFs have been integrated into a device. 40 Much work needs to be done to achieve fast, accurate and versatile patterning techniques with MOFs. The need to control their growth/position on a variety of substrates and the ability to tune the crystal size, orientation and quality are currently major challenges in the field. This progress is crucial to exploit MOF properties as part of integrated platforms.
An illustrative perspective of a complex microfluidic MOF based device is presented in Figure 1a, where the multifunctional ultra-porous crystal can act as a separator, catalyst and reservoir on a single microchip. The ability to combine MOF spatial control with the capacity to precisely locate exogenous functional species within the ultraporous frameworks would offer additional versatility for MOF based device fabrication (a representation of a microreactor utilising MOF crystals is illustrated in Figure 1b).
Here we review the recent progress in the control of metal organic framework position/growth on different substrates as well as mastering the location of active functional materials inside ultra-porous crystals. Different synthetic protocols, crucial aspects, remarkable distinctive features achieved by each lithographic approach are classified and critically discussed with the intention of stimulating further progress in this emerging research area. Both the methodology to control the MOF positioning on substrates and the MOF functionalization within the porous crystals are discussed. The manuscript includes an extensive examination of the future outlook, proposing several promising techniques used for the controlled growth and positioning of ceramic/organic materials which have not yet been applied to MOF fabrication.
