## 2. Methods for the Spatial Control of MOF Positioning
Self-assembly presents a spontaneous mechanism for the preparation of two- and three-dimensional ordered structures. This synthetic approach, related to MOF formation, offers the advantage to build up a framework with defined porosity without the need for further processing. 40  Other classes of porous materials prepared via self assembly techniques such as mesoporous oxides generally require the removal of a templating agent. 60–62 However, due to the intrinsic nature of the spontaneous organization, control over the growth and position of the MOF selfassembly presents a challenge. 59
While for other self-assembled materials, fabrication methods have combined bottom up and top down processes, 26 the current MOF patterning protocols have been exclusively based on a bottom-up approach. Although nature has provided evidence that it is possible to control crystallization processes carefully 63  and progress for the use of self-assembly for device fabrication has been successfully achieved, 64  in general, the ability to master MOF growth requires further improvement. In fact, what would be ideally required by a fabrication technique which takes full advantage of MOF functionality is control over the crystal size, position, orientation and lattice quality in a fast and inexpensive process offering strong adhesion between the porous material and the substrate.
The majority of the protocols described in the review use different strategies to induce the MOF nucleation/growth in specific locations or to control the ultra-porous crystal position. Here, we have identified three different methods:
Fabrication methods involving promoting agents  this fabrication method directly involves the control of the MOF position using a specific agent (e.g. seeding method or controlled film growth). Such an agent plays an active role enhancing/promoting the MOF nucleation/growth process in selected areas.
Fabrication methods requiring external control: an external control is used to drive the nucleation in a confined space. The external control can be any external driving force promoting the growth of MOFs (e.g. electrochemical approach) or controlling/ depositing the amount of MOF precursors in precise locations (e.g. microcontact printing and evaporation induced growth).
Fabrication methods that require external control to position preformed MOFs  the use of an external control to position already synthesized MOFs. With this two-step approach (synthesis and relocation) the positioning can be dynamically adjusted (e.g. patterning using a magnetic field) and the films can be formed from colloidal MOF solutions.
## 2.1. Fabrication Methods Involving Promoting Agents
## 2.1.1. Controlled Thin Film Growth
As the focus of MOF development shifts from the design of new high surface area materials into the application of these materials, the ability to control the formation and properties of MOF films is critical. Control of the crystal orientation, thickness, uniformity and functionality is required if MOF films are to be used for applications such as chemical sensing, 42  membrane separation 30  and storage. 39  Several reviews on the formation of MOF films have recently been published; 53 54 65  however, here we focus on the ability to position and pattern MOFs for device applications.
Positioning and patterning MOF films generally requires pre-treatment and functionalization of the substrate. Thin MOF films were initially anchored to surfaces with Self-Assembled organic Monolayers $\mathrm{(SAMs^{[66]})}$ via solvothermal means. 67  The MOF-5 building units were bound to the SAM’s carboxylic acid surface groups forming a $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick polycrystalline film which was selectively patterned 67  in combination with soft lithographic microcontact printing $(\mathsf{\upmu C P}^{[11]})$ (Figure 2a). It has been shown that the SAM layer is critical for producing preferential orientation of the MOF crystals within the thin films. 68–70 Bein et al. showed that the out-of-plane crystal orientation can change depending on whether the SAM is terminated by the COOH- or the OH- groups. 68
Fischer’s group 71–74  further controlled the MOF film crystallisation through a layer-by-layer $(\mathrm{LbL}^{[75]})$ or liquid phase epitaxy $(\mathrm{LPE}^{[76]})$ technique in which the ultrathin MOF film (termed SURMOF) is built by immersing the substrate into alternate solutions of the metal precursor and the organic ligand, producing preferentially oriented crystallinity in the out-of-plane direction (Figure 2b). These films are in the nanometer range of thickness and are built up in steps, allowing the formation of MOFs, some of which are not able to be formed via the more conventional bulk synthesis techniques. The resultant LbL films have a very smooth surface (roughness in the order of a few molecules) due to the precise formation process. 65
Further progress producing in-plane order as well as out of plane order was made through layer-by-layer synthesis using the Langmuir Blodgett technique where the preparation of well ordered monolayers on a liquid surface can be deposited onto the solid MOF interface using precursor molecules with hydrophilic and hydrophobic end groups. 77 78  Kitagawa et al. 77 78  formed highly crystalline MOF nanofilms using porphyrin molecular building blocks with metal-ion coordinates under mild conditions and on both gold and silicon substrates. These films offer potential as gas sensors due to their ‘gating’ ability to structurally change from a closed structure to an open porous structure upon selective sorption of gases.
The ability to control the position of ordered crystalline MOF thin films has promoted a flurry of activity using these porous materials for industrial applications. There is great potential for highly crystalline porous uniform thin films as sensing devices when the patterned films can be functionalised with active and adaptive molecules. 79–82  A recent application requiring controlled crystal growth involves the formation of MOF surface coated chromatography capillaries for gas separation. 83 Using a carboxylic terminated SAM, MOF-5 ( $\mathrm{\ZnO_{4}}$ tetrahedra connected with benzene dicarboxylate linkers) 27  was built up on the inner surface of the capillary using the layer-by-layer approach where the inorganic coupling units and linker precursor solutions were alternatively pumped through between washing and drying steps. This method allowed the controlled formation of a crystalline MOF film without blocking the capillary due to excess crystal growth, and the device performed well for the separation of natural gas components. 83
Terfort’s group 84 85  recently produced patterned MOF thin films using the layer-by layer growth technique on SAM functionalised surfaces. The Cu-ADA MOF films, prepared from Cu-carboxylate dimer secondary building units (SBUs) and 4,4-azobenzene dicarboxylate ligands, were grown on gold substrates patterned with a 1-hexadecanethiol (HDT) SAM via the microcontact printing $\mathrm{(\upmuCP)^{[86]}}$ fabrication method. SEM showed localised preferential crystal growth of Cu-ADA on the HDT SAM surface leaving the surrounding bare gold surface clear (Figure 2c). Interestingly, the $\mathtt{C u}$ -ADA MOF would only grow on the disordered HDT $\upmu\mathrm{CP}$ deposited SAM surface and not on an ordered HDT surface, again highlighting the importance of the substrate surface for the development and quality of MOF crystallisation. 84
The advantage of layer-by-layer synthesis is that it can produce MOF thin films with crystal ordering in both dimensions, across and out of the substrate. The thickness of the films is well controlled by the number of cycles completed during fabrication and the position of the MOF films can then be determined through patterning of the SAM combined with a $\upmu\mathrm{CP}$ fabrication technique. However there are a number of limitations to this protocol, the first being the lengthy synthesis due to the repeated cycles required to build up films which can take several days. Automated high throughput equipment has recently been used to speed up the synthesis using a spray method to form patterned HKUST- $1^{[87]}$ MOFs ( $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(1,3,5\mathrm{-}$ benzenetricarboxylate)2) on a $\upmu\mathrm{CP}$ SAM substrate. 88  Another consideration is that the entire deposition process requires a controlled atmosphere if the MOF under preparation is sensitive to atmospheric conditions (e.g. humidity). The quality of the patterning of these MOF thin films is dependent on the ability to pattern the SAM using the $\upmu\mathrm{CP}$ method and is therefore limited by inherent SAM temperature and chemical sensitivities. In addition, several MOFs cannot be prepared using the LbL method due to their specific preparation requirements such as high formation temperatures and pressures and varied chemical requirements such as $\mathrm{\pH}$ levels, surfactants, inert atmosphere and specific solvents.
