## 2.1.2. Seeding Method
Fast, cheap and scalable production of MOF-based devices must rely on synthetic procedures capable of growing MOF crystals on a variety of substrates. Methods to reduce the dependence of MOF production processes on rate limiting synthesis parameters are in demand. Recent efforts have been dedicated to the development of MOF preparation methods that aim to reduce the device production time while increasing the MOF synthesis flexibility. In this context, the concept of using nucleation seeds to induce controlled MOF formation has been successfully implemented to achieve MOF formation on a variety of substrates and substrate geometries. The nucleation seeds can be nano- or micro- particles of MOF itself (“homogeneous” seeding), but also examples of MOF grown using nano- or micro- seeds of metal oxides and phosphates have been reported (“heterogeneous” seeding).
Caro and co-workers 89  provide an example of controlled ZIF-7 ((Zn(benzimidazolate $\lvert_{2}\rvert^{[90]}$ growth promoted on ZIF-7 seeds. Zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) are a sub-family of MOFs which consist of transition metal ions and imidazolate linkers forming tetrahedral frameworks which resemble zeolite-type porous structures. 90  Caro et al. 89  initially observed that diethylamine allows for the controlled growth of ZIF-7 rods when $\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}$ is used as the precursor of the framework metal centres. They successfully applied this observation to induce directional growth of ZIF-7 membranes from layers of randomly oriented ZIF-7 nanoparticles deposited on an alumina substrate. Their protocol relies on the initial synthesis of ZIF-7 nanoparticles $(40-140\mathrm{nm})$ ) through a conventional solvothermal method in the presence of polyethylene imine. 89  The viscous colloidal suspension can be used directly to deposit layers of particles on alumina substrates by multiple dip-coatings. The seeded substrates subsequently undergo a secondary growth step in a DMF solution of $\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}$ benzimidazole and diethylamine, through a microwave assisted autoclave treatment at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The membrane starts to show a defined orientation along the c (out-of-plane) axis after 45 minutes, and its thickness is observed to increase nearly linearly with the treatment time, indicating an absence of further nucleation. The authors demonstrated that the membrane is homogeneous and dense by measuring a $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas separation factor of 8.4 at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . 89 Once a controlled process to prepare small MOFs seeds has been achieved, the next step to attain spatial control is related to the control of the seed position.
The “heterogeneous” seeding of MOF crystals has been recently reported. 37 91 92  Buso et al. 92  described the fast nucleation of mono-dispersed MOF-5 microcrystals obtained using carboxy- and amino-functionalized silica nanoparticles as nucleation seeds. They observed that MOF-5 formation occurs ten times faster than the conventional solvothermal synthesis when using silica seeds under stirring conditions, reducing the MOF-5 crystal formation time from tens of hours down to a few hours. Beds of MOF-5 crystals were successfully grown on localized areas of flat silicon substrates by drop casting a suspension of functionalized silica seeds. 91  This approach allows preferential MOF growth in areas defined by the location of the particles and several lithographic techniques are currently available for precise particle positioning. 93–95  Variables such as solvents, substrates, and atmosphere can be used to control the self assembly of silica based particles in precise locations. 96
In general, the ceramic seeds should have a longer shelflife than small MOF crystals for homogeneous nucleation (as the majority of MOFs are moisture sensitive); however, a dry storage atmosphere is preferable to maintain the nucleation properties (e.g. amino functionalized silica nanoparticles) due to the affinity of some functional groups (e.g. amines) with water. 97  Falcaro et al. 37  and Buso et al. 92  report on a novel class of $\upalpha$ -hopeite microparticles as effective nucleating seeds for the fast formation of MOF-5, which show several advantages over the above mentioned functionalized silica particles. Named Desert Rose Microparticles (DRMs), these seeds form spontaneously within the starting MOF-5 precursor solution when Pluronic F-127 is added. Overall, the growth kinetics of MOF-5 in the presence of the DRMs is three times faster than the traditional solvothermal approach. The micrometre size DRMs can be effectively isolated from the precursor solution and used as seeds to induce localized MOF growth on a variety of substrates. MOF formation on DRM seeds is independent of both the substrate surface chemistry and the substrate geometry. The authors successfully grew MOF-5 crystals on flat silicon and alumina substrates, and also on more complex 3D geometries, by controlling the location of the micron seeds. The 3D geometries were achieved using a commercial resist SU-8 membrane previously lithographed with arrays of vertical microchannels using Deep X-Ray Lithography. 98  DRMs were introduced inside each one of the lithographed channels, and the whole assembly was immersed in a MOF-5 precursor solution. MOF-5 crystals were observed to preferentially form inside the channels throughout the thickness of the membrane (Figure 3). 37
This methodology can be combined with a lithographic approach for the control of the seed position. Although further studies are needed to fully understand the potential of the heterogeneous seeding technique, it represents an alternative methodology which could offer the opportunity to use inexpensive inorganic particles to induce controlled MOF growth.
The current limitations associated with this technique are the loss of surface area induced by the inorganic seed occupancy, the lack of preferential orientation for the crystal growth, and lower nucleation efficiency when compared with small MOF seeds. The advantages are lower moisture sensitivity, the availability of lithographic methodologies to pattern circuits with nano and microparticles, the compatibility with different substrates and the short MOF patterning fabrication time (e.g. Using DRMs MOF-5 growth is approximately 3 times faster than regular solvothermal growth). Another potential advantage of the seeding method is the capability of incorporating functionality within the seeds. This aspect is discussed further in Section 3.3.
## 2.2. Fabrication Methods Requiring External Control
## 2.2.1. Electrochemical Approach
Electrochemical investigations of Cu- and Zn-MOFs were first reported in $2007^{[99]}$ using an electrochemical based MOF and growth of MOF crystals is enhanced in such areas, as depicted in Figure 4 As a consequence, a continuous coating of MOF can be prepared. Films with thickness in the 1 to $20~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ range were obtained with a height variation measured within 50 and $20\%$ respectively; the thicker the film, the better the control of the thickness while the roughness of the coating depends on the crystal size distribution.
Ameloot and co-workers 101  have shown the ability of this straightforward technique to control MOF coating formation on top of a quartz crystal microbalance. This microsystem has been used successfully as a relative humidity sensor and is the first experiment providing evidence that an electrochemical method can be combined with standard lithographic techniques for the preparation of microelectronic circuits to master MOF growth in controlled locations.
