Further progress with this technique was presented by the same authors in $2010^{[102]}$ where controlled evaporative conditions 103 were combined with the electrochemical procedure. A crucial fabrication step was the substrate preparation. A glass slide (substrate) was chemically functionalized with an alkylsilane (chlorotrimethylsilane); silanes with hydrophobic functionalities are widely used to induce a locally controlled chemical wettability on silica based substrates. 24 18 104  Subsequently, a mask was applied on top of the methyl-functionalized silica slide and a metal evaporation deposition process was used to fabricate a copper pattern. The ligand (BTC) and silver nitrate were dissolved in dimethyl sulphide (DMSO) and then spin-coated on top of the patterned substrate. A preferential dewetting of the DMSO solution was detected in the methyl functionalized regions; the solution mainly wet the metallic regions giving the opportunity to perform the electrochemical reaction in defined areas of $50\times50~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (microreactors). Remarkably, the roughness of silver deposited during the electrochemical process ensures robust anchorage of the 100–200 nm $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ MOF crystals. 107
The current limitation associated with this technique is related to the need for a metallic support. Although the fabricated MOF based microstructures have demonstrated promising mechanical properties (e.g. the MOF pattern is resistant to ultrasound treatments), further durability tests could confirm important features such as hardness as well as scratch and peeling resistance of the coating. Further studies are likely to highlight that the resolution achievable using electrochemically deposited patterns may be limited due to crystal growth.
