## 2.2.2. Microcontact Printing and Evaporation Induced Growth
The strive towards industrially viable methods to produce MOFs is highlighted by numerous efforts to achieve spatial and temporal control of MOF formation and growth, particularly on selected supports. There are a variety of parameters that can be controlled to tune MOF formation, and it is essential to identify those that are compatible with new and flexible types of processing. In this vein, MOF synthesis techniques have increasingly been extended beyond the standard batch solvothermal approach, which are generally limited to a strict set of precursors, concentration, temperature, etc. Recently, increased attention to the role played by the solvent in the control of MOF formation kinetics has led to effective synthesis methodologies based on evaporation induced MOF formation. This technique has been extensively used for mesoporous film preparation and it is called evaporation induced self-assembly (EISA). 106 The evaporation induced approach has been successfully coupled to conventional microprinting and dip-pen lithography, providing effective means to trigger controlled growth of MOF single crystals on specific substrate locations with micron precision in twodimensions.
crystals is obtained on the metallic surfaces and the scanning microscope images provide evidence that crystal growth is confined to the defined regions. The technique could possibly be applied to a variety of MOFs using a suitable lithographed metal pattern. Furthermore, lithographic methods for the fabrication of complex metallic micro- and nano-structures are widely available. Importantly, in regards to lithography, electrochemical methods are suitable for the preparation of a variety of metallic micro- and nano-structures in the first fabrication step; 105  therefore, the same technique can be used to grow the MOFs on such structures. This fabrication method can be considered inexpensive (due to the limited amount of chemicals used) and versatile and is therefore very promising for industrial applications.
Ameloot et al. 107  managed to inhibit the room temperature nucleation of HKUST-1 by replacing the standard ethanol/water solvent with dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO). DMSO shows stronger affinity towards metal ions and tends to form hydrogen bonded solvate structures with trimesic acid ligands, thus stabilizing the precursor solution at room temperature. The authors discovered that the stabilizing effect is fully reversible allowing them to trigger HKUST-1 formation by solvent evaporation at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ during the conventional solvothermal route. The authors used this novel synthesis route to induce formation of MOF crystals on specific substrate locations by means of microcontact printing. The method was accomplished using the “lithographically controlled wetting” $\big(\mathrm{LCW}^{[108]}\big)$ technique. The protrusions of a microlithographed polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) stamp are inked with the MOF precursor solution, and the stamp is subsequently placed on the target substrate (Figure 5a–d). Capillary forces will maintain the solution exclusively underneath the stamp protrusions, which is the key to confine MOF growth on the substrate. MOF formation can then be induced by controlled evaporation at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Individual MOF crystals have been successfully deposited along a modular pattern by using an appropriate stamp protrusion feature size $(2\times2~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ (Figure 5a–d). The authors also observed that the physical confinement of the precursor solution between the substrate and the stamp seems to impose a preferred orientation on the HKUST-1 crystals along the [111] direction, irrespective of the substrate surface chemistry. In fact, the same crystal orientation was observed in the case of silanol, vinyl or carboxylic acid substrate surface groups. The authors managed to demonstrate that evaporation is the main driving force for nucleation over the process temperature, by showing that no crystal formation occurs under the same temperature conditions when the process is performed in a closed vessel. The technique has successfully been applied to grow ordered arrays of ZIF- $8^{[90]}$ and MOF-5 single crystals. 107
Evaporation induced MOF growth offers the possibility of controlling MOF crystal formation by tuning the volume of the precursor solution. When this is done on a substrate, controlled growth of MOF single crystals can be achieved with high spatial accuracy. Bellido 109  and Carbonell 110  present remarkable examples of how to use the tip of an atomic force microscope (AFM) or a scanning tunnelling microscope (STM), to deposit femtoliter droplets of MOF precursor solution onto specified locations on SAM functionalized substrates (Figure 6e–h). This deposition technique is called dip-pen nanolithography. 111  The micron droplets act as micro-incubators for MOF nucleation and allow for the deposition of MOF crystal arrays. Their work is also based on the controlled nucleation of HKUST-1 at room temperature, made possible by substituting the traditional ethanol/water solvent mixture with DMSO. 110
Bellido et al. 109  managed to grow HKUST-1 crystals at specific locations on self-assembled monolayers of 16- mercaptohexadecanoic acid and 1-octanethiol deposited on gold substrates. They found that the crystal growth can be tuned by controlling the solvent evaporation rate through incubation of the substrates in a closed vessel saturated with solvent vapours. The obtained crystal diameters range from 150 to $650~\mathrm{nm}$ . 109 Although the controlled deposition of a single crystal per droplet of each solution had not been achieved, the experiment demonstrates the potential of this technique to obtain crystal arrays with a spatial resolution of microns.
On the other hand, Carbonell et al. 110  deposited arrays of MOF precursor solution droplets on a substrate using a STM tip and found that the contact angle of the droplets is a critical factor in obtaining controlled precipitation of a MOF single
