crystal per droplet. The authors investigated the effect of different surface chemistries on the precipitation dynamics of MOF crystals from the microdroplets and observed that precipitation of single crystals can be induced by increasing the droplet contact angle. They found that– ${\mathrm{.CF}}_{3}$ and– $\mathrm{.CH}_{3}$ functionalised surfaces are ideal for precipitation of MOF single crystals from each of the microdroplets, whereas more hydrophilic surface groups, i.e. $\mathrm{-NH}_{3}$ –COOH and–OH, tend to increase the MOF nucleation density on the contact surface. 110  The authors tuned the droplet contact angle and obtained ordered arrays of HKUST-1 single crystals with average edge dimensions between $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $1.2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Precise control over the edge dimension of the single crystals can be achieved by varying the volume of the microdroplets. This volume control is obtained by tailoring the plasma treatment time of the STM tip, which modifies the tip’s hydrophilicity to allow for deposition of different volumes of solution. The authors also observed that HKUST-1 single crystals tend to grow preferentially oriented along their [111] direction when forming on hydrophobic– $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ and– ${\mathrm{.CF}}_{3}$ self-assembled monolayers (SAMs), 110  which complements the observations of Wöll et al. 112  and Bein et al. 68  made for MOFs grown on hydrophilic–OH and–COOH SAMs. Although dip-pen nanolithography is highly precise, this patterning technique is normally considered time expensive; recent progress has shown the advantage of a parallel probe for high throughput deposition. 113
You and co-workers 114  combined the possibility to depolymerise and re-polymerise coordination polymers with a standard method of micromolding in capillaries (MIMIC 115 ), and managed to create bi-dimensional micro-arrangements of highly luminescent reticular superstructures on a silica substrate. In general, coordination polymers can be reversibly depolymerised by dissolving them in a strong coordination solvent. Subsequently, they can be reconfigured into their initial macrostructure by controlled removal of the solvent. When this reversible process is performed within the microcavities of a lithographed micromould, microstructures of the coordination polymer reproducing the mould patterns can be obtained. 114 Zinc coordinated $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -di(4-pyridyl)cyanostilbene was initially dissolved in an excess of pyridine, to obtain a clear solution. The solution was then introduced into rectangular capillaries in conformal contact with a patterned polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) mould and a glass substrate. The excess pyridine was observed to diffuse into the PDMS walls, inducing re-polymerisation of the zinc coordinated $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -di(4-pyridyl)cyanostilbene within the microcapillaries. The PDMS mould was eventually peeled off the substrate, leaving ordered coordination polymer microstructures on the silica substrate. The authors observed that the polymer microstructures reproduced the surface of the capillaries with high precision and that grooves and shrinkage were minimal. Also, the fabricated patterns showed strong birefringence under cross-polarized illumination conditions, indicating that the patterns had a pronounced directional order. 114
Gassensmith et al. 116  were able to pattern the surface of a ${\sim}0.5\mathrm{mm}$ single MOF-5 crystal using a stamp formed by $\upmu\mathrm{CP}$ of functionalised silicon wafer surfaces. The advantage of this technique is that the functionalised alkylazide surfaces are more robust compared to the traditional gold-thiol SAM initially used for MOF film formation. This layer can be further functionalised using $\upmu\mathrm{CP}$ to create patterned MOF surfaces. The authors first prepared the azide-terminated monolayer which was then patterned with a PDMS stamp loaded with copper-catalyzed alkyne-azide cycloaddition (CuAAC). The resulting carboxylic acid pattern provides a nucleating surface for MOF growth once the substrate is immersed in MOF-5 growing medium. The resulting substrates showed $40\mathrm{nm}$ thick uniform MOF films on the carboxyl-terminated surfaces and no MOF formation on the azide surfaces. Continued immersion in the MOF-5 growing medium, formed MOF film monoliths which could be removed from the substrate and featured the patterned surface. 116
The current limitation associated with this technique is mainly related to the low amount of MOF that can be prepared for each patterning process. Furthermore, the technique does not seem to be easily applied to complex 3D substrates. Advantages include the low cost associated with the patterning methodology and the relatively low amount of reactants involved in the process. Fine control of the crystal orientation has been achieved, and the high precision and straightforward methodology, referring in particular to the work of Ameloot, 107  offers the opportunity to pattern large surfaces.
## 2.3. Fabrication Methods that Require External Control to Position Preformed MOFs
## 2.3.1. Magnetic Patterning
Magnetic functionalization of MOFs is a promising method for spatial manipulation and has the added attribute enabling a stimuli responsive material. The ability to spatially control and pattern a variety of materials using an external magnetic field has previously been accomplished with superparamagnetic magnetite micro- or nano-particles, 117–120  Ni microparticles, 121 peptide nanotubes 122  and human endothelial progenitor cell via iron oxide nanoparticle labelling. 123  Initial fabrication strategies for magnetic MOF materials involved the synthesis of MOF crystals with the use of magnetic precursor elements such as Mn, Co and Fe as the coordination metals. 124–128  Despite the demonstrated remarkable magnetic properties, no evidence of spatial control has been reported for these magnetic MOF crystals. Subsequent studies have focused on the formation of MOF-magnetic particle composites. 36 129 131
Lohe et al. 129  recently reported the preparation and use of magnetically functionalised MOF particles for drug delivery and catalytic applications. In this study, the authors were able to integrate superparamagnetic functionality by preparing iron oxide nanoparticle-MOF composites which featured catalytic functionality. The magnetically functionalised MOFs were prepared via a two-step approach where the surface of the $10{-}20\mathrm{nm}$ iron oxide nanoparticles were initially grafted with the MOF precursors in order to heterogeneously nucleate the growth of MOF crystals. The resulting AlOH(2,6-naphthalenedicarboxylate) 130  magnetic framework composites were used as a catalyst in the cyanosilylation of benzaldehyde to produce cyanohydrins for synthesis of pharmaceuticals. 129  Magnetic separation of the catalyst improved the efficiency of catalyst recovery over traditional filtration methods. The authors also used the magnetic MOF composites to promote desorption of stored drug molecules. Magnetic $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ composites were loaded with ibuprofen within the porous framework. Using an alternating magnetic field the composite framework heated up due to the superparamagnetic nature of the particles which resulted in the release of the drug. 129  This methodology offers both magnetic spatial control and controlled drug delivery.
Ke et al. 131  prepared $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/C u_{3}(B T C)_{2}}$ framework composites for targeted drug delivery also providing a controlled release mechanism. The composites were prepared with $50{-}150\ \mathrm{nm}$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanorods embedded within the HKUST-1 MOF structure which provided an open porous network to store nimesulide, a cancer drug for pancreatic cancer. Superparamagnetism is an essential property for magnetic biomaterials used for medical applications, such as drug delivery, as the low residual magnetisation prevents particle agglomeration which would cause undesirable embolisms. 131
Other framework composites (FC) prepared with $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ carbon-coated cobalt particles were recently used by Falcaro et al. 36  for the spatial control of MOF-5 growth using an external magnetic field. These high surface area porous FCs featured both luminescent properties as well as the ability for spatial manipulation via external magnetic fields. The FCs were prepared using a one-pot procedure in which the carbon coated cobalt nanoparticles were added to the MOF-5 growing medium along with a surfactant (Pluronic, F127) to prevent premature sedimentation. After 24 hours of thermal treatment, dark cubic crystals formed and the location of the resulting magnetic FCs could be manoeuvred with the use of a magnetic field. The authors also demonstrated that they were able to direct the location of the MOF crystal growth using static magnetic fields to collect the Co FCs (Figure 6). The magnetic FCs seeds were attracted to areas of strong magnetic field (tips of the magnets) where the Co FCs continued growing when placed in a fresh Co MOF-5 growth medium. 36
The ability to control both the growth position as well as spatially manipulate the location of the MOF crystals is promising for industrial applications and device fabrication. The possibility to prepare magnetic MOFs with a one-pot synthesis together with the potential to relocate the ultra-porous crystals within a device provide significant advantages for this patterning technique. The small loss of surface area $(4\%)$ due to the presence of the magnetic particles offers promise for applications utilising the uptake and storage properties of the MOF crystals. The main disadvantage of this method is the requirement for the use of strong micromagnets or lithographed electromagnets for positioning the MOFs. Due to the interaction between the magnetic crystals and magnetic fields, the ability to position separated single crystals with controlled orientation represents a challenge. Currently, the ability to position single MOF crystals has not yet been demonstrated using any external field.
## 2.3.2. Films from Colloidal MOF Solutions
An alternative method of preparing MOF films is from colloidal solutions containing preformed MOF crystals which has been reported by Ferey and Sanchez et al. 56–58  This relatively fast technique of depositing MOF crystals via dip-coating allows good control over the film thickness $(40\ \mathrm{nm}-1\ \upmu\mathrm{m})$ . Stable, homogeneous, mono-dispersed colloidal solutions of MOFs (MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ , 132  MIL-89 (Fe), 133  ZIF-8 $(Z\mathrm{n})^{[90]})$ were prepared via controlled hydrothermal means. The film thickness was carefully defined by both the withdrawal speed of the dip-coater as well as the number of depositions (with washing and drying steps between each coating). Each deposition step forms one or two MOF layers, therefore the smoothness and optical properties of the films are optimised through the careful formation of the preformed mono-dispersed MOF nanoparticles. The resulting films showed high optical quality with good transparency and optical transmittance greater than $96\%$ , confirming the homogeneous deposition of the nanoparticles. 56–58 134  Such a technique has been efficiently used for the fabrication of freestanding membranes and sensors. 135  The advantage of these films is that they can be produced relatively quickly and the thickness can be carefully tailored; however, they feature polydispersed crystallinity as opposed to the more controlled but slower layer-by-layer thin film formation technique. Although the deposition technique is very promising, patterned MOF substrates have not yet been fabricated using this method and more research is needed in this field for better insight into the related advantages and limitations of the technique.
## 3. Methods to Control Functionalities Inside MOFs
The ability to control functional properties and the position of functional particles inside MOF crystals would allow for the fabrication of a novel class of host-guest materials that can take mutual advantage of both the guest functional particles and the host framework porous properties.
Although a variety of studies have reported different methods to embed different functional materials within MOFs, in this review we include only those methods providing evidence of spatial control. Controlling the position of functional species within a MOF crystal is essential for the development of new MOF based devices which require molecular activity (e.g. selective gating photonic sensors, catalysis targeting specific molecules, microreactors pre-concentrating active molecules, molecular sieving etc.)
## 3.1. Direct Laser Writing
In 2011 De Vos’s group 136  presented an elegant patterning protocol based on direct laser writing to fabricate patterns of metallic nanoparticles within a single MOF crystal (Figure 7). A photoactive framework, such as MOF-5 was chosen for the proof of concept. MOF-5 presents numerous advantages for these experiments: the crystal lattice is made of photoactive inorganic centers spaced by linkers acting as antennae; the radiation is effectively absorbed through a two-photon absorption process (this multiphoton absorption allows for confined photoreduction in the focal volume of the laser beam, where irradiation is most intense) 137   the MOF-5 porosity has defined and uniform features and the 3D interconnected cavity arrangement allow for the diffusion of a metal precursor during the fabrication step.
A single MOF-5 crystal was positioned on top of a silica glass slide. A $10~\mathrm{mM}$ ethanol solution of silver nitrate $(\mathrm{AgNO}_{3})$ was used to provide the metal precursor during the irradiation with a femtosecond pulsed $780\mathrm{-nm}$ laser. The laser pulses were focused between 10 and 60 microns inside the crystal. Pixels of about $750~\mathrm{nm}$ were typically obtained after irradiating the embedded crystals for $100{-}250~\mathrm{ms}$ . An example of a 2D circuit patterned $45\mathrm{~x~}45\upmu\mathrm{m}$ is presented in Figure 7 and the black dots (cluster of silver particles) can be easily detected. 136
research group 138  have efficiently combined MOFs with a wet stamping technique. 139 MOF-5 and CD-MOF-2 (a MOF prepared with $\upgamma$ cyclodextrin and rubidium hydroxide 140 ) have been used. The interconnected porosity of these frameworks and the established protocols for crystal preparation on the millimetre scale for these MOFs are considered crucial pre-requisites; the porosity allows diffusion through the framework and the ability to prepare big crystals makes it possible to use stamps with micron features. Stamps made of furfurylamido-bisphenol A diglycidyl ether (FA-BADE) organogel have been used for MOF-5 lithography while agarose has been used for CD-MOF-2. The stamps were soaked with a variety of different dyes and the required solvent (FA-BADE soaked in dimethyl formammide and agarose in methanol, respectively) for a few hours to embed the dye prior to use (Figure 8). The times involved in the printing process for the fabrication of clearly visible patterns were $3{-}30\ \operatorname{min}$ for CD-MOF-2 compared with $0.16{-}2\mathrm{min}$ for MOF-5. Example of dyes successfully used for MOF printing are: blue (MB), brilliant green (BG), pyronin B (PB), pyronin Y (PY), thionin (TH), toluidine blue O (TBO), azure A (AA), azure B (AB), azure C (AC), rhodamine B (RB), methyl yellow (MY), methyl orange (MO), methyl red (MR),
Lithographic processes can be used to fabricate complex 3D metallic nanoparticle arrays or patterns within previously prepared MOF-5 crystals which have been infiltrated with the metal precursor solution. Although these microfabrication protocols are promising for microelectronic, sensing, anti-counterfeiting and other smart device applications, further studies are needed to investigate properties such as the conductivity of the metallic pattern as it is important to ensure good electrical contact between the photo-generated particles of the metallic pattern and the external electrical circuit. The technique relies on a patterning process (positioning, alignment and laser writing) that would significantly impact the cost of the final device; however, it is currently the only method allowing both 2D and 3D metallic patterns to be written inside a MOF crystal.
## 3.2. Modified Wet Stamping
Due to the technological need to fabricate responsive and adaptive ultraporous materials targeting specific stimuli, Grzybowski’s
