1-cyclopentene (diarylethene) or a mixture of these. 139
Using this suggested protocol, a $\mathrm{\pH}$ indicator pattern has been prepared (e.g. MO) for detecting the presence of ammonia or hydrochloric acid. 138  Another successful proof of concept has been reported showing the patterning of ultraporous crystals with photochromic dye (diaryl ethene). The pattern changes from transparent to blue when irradiated with UV light $(254\mathrm{nm})$ . The response to the aforementioned stimuli can be cycled a number of times depending on the combination of: processes involved, MOF type and adsorbed molecule (10 cycles were performed for the acid/base sensing and more than 300 cycles for the photochromic detection).
The process is a straightforward and cheap method for positioning functional organic and inorganic species within MOFs. The limitations of this process are given by the need to have functional species compatible with the framework in terms of chemical stability and dimension to diffuse into the crystals. The region of the crystal close to the surface is more likely to be patterned than the inner MOF volume; this limitation can represent a possible advantage offering the possibility to functionalize a single crystal with a different functional pattern on each side as well as the ability to create functional gradients. In fact, using a single crystal, a multiresponsive porous platform could be fabricated by patterning each crystal face with different stimuli-responsive molecules or nanoparticles.
## 3.3. Controlled Self Assembly Processes on functionalized Seeding Agents
The ability to control the self-assembly process, forming a porous framework around functional materials, presents another possibility to prepare multifunctional porous materials. Different studies have been performed in order to embed exogenous species in MOFs; 35 129 141  however, only a few studies have been able to achieve control over positioning functional materials within the ultraporous crystals. 32 142  These approaches involve a two-step process where the initial functional material is prepared first and subsequently the porous framework is grown around the functional material.
Microcarriers acting simultaneously as nucleation seeds can be used to introduce functional species within MOF-5 crystals. This strategy, proposed by Falcaro et al. 37  takes advantage of DRMs synthesized in the presence of functional nanoparticles. The functionalized microparticles can then be embedded within MOF-5 and in optimized conditions one single DRM particle can be enclosed within each single MOF-5 crystal. Using functionalized DRM seeds, the authors obtained composites with a MOF-5 host matrix containing catalytic metal nanoparticles (Pt and Pd), highly luminescent semiconductor Quantum Dots (QDs) (CdSe-CdS-ZnS) or polymer nanoparticles (Teflon). The porosity and surface area of the MOF-5 host remained unchanged despite the presence of the functional hosts. The QDs/DRM/MOF-5 composites were tested as luminescent molecular sieves using an emission quenching test. The test showed that the MOF-5 host framework effectively inhibits the diffusion of large quenching agents (thiols), thus leaving the embedded QDs emission properties unaltered. Only quenching molecules with sizes smaller than the MOF-5 pores could diffuse through the host matrix and eventually quench the QD luminescence. 37  This work demonstrates that using DRM seeds, the actual position of the functional nano-species can be precisely controlled within each single MOF crystal, with no trace of the guest functionality on the outer surface of the MOF matrix. 37  From a practical point of view, the DRM procedure for the synthesis of MOF-5 composites is independent of the chemical nature of the functional species. Lee et al. 142 prepared a core-shell MOF composite using carboxylate-terminated polystyrene particles $(\sim1~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ diameter) in the presence of 2-methylimidazole and $\mathrm{{Zn}(N O_{3})_{2}}$ After $10\ \mathrm{min}$ at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ a $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick shell of ZIF- $8^{[90]}$ can be detected. Although the authors use the polymeric particles as a template for the preparation of hollowed MOF spheres, the encapsulation of a functional doping agent inside the polymeric particles could potentially offer a route to control the position of functional materials within core-shell MOF particles.
## 4. Conclusion and Future Outlook
New research trends in MOF technology have shown the potential to bring these ultra-porous frameworks into the realm of device fabrication. Pioneering activity in patterning methodologies is very recent, and in the past few years important progress has been made in identifying different routes/mechanisms to control the position of MOFs on substrates. These different methods rely on: positioning promoting agents to induce preferential growth (chemical functionalization or nucleation seeds); inducing growth through the use of external controls (electrochemistry, microcontact); positioning preprepared stimuli-responsive ultra-porous crystals using an external field (e.g. magnetic). Progress showing the potential to embed functional species and control their position within a single crystal has been illustrated via laser writing, wet stamping in preformed MOFs, or controlled self assembly on a functional seed.
Features that need to be controlled or investigated  Despite the variety of approaches available for positioning MOFs on a surface, each protocol can successfully address several specific needs. Nevertheless further advances are required to satisfy important device criteria simultaneously. Indeed, the ideal protocol would be a fast, cheap and versatile methodology for the control of the MOF position, dimension and crystal orientation/ quality. Interestingly, similar important parameters have been identified by other researchers working on patterning processes for different crystalline materials. For instance, Whitesides’s group 143  identified in 1999 some practical challenges that need to be addressed to achieve control over crystallization for the fabrication of inorganic crystalline-based advanced materials. The important key parameters are control over the density and pattern nucleation events as well as the size and orientation of the growing crystals. On the other hand, to control the crystal formation of organic semiconductors (e.g. pentacene, rubene and fullerene) Briseno et al. 144  recognized the necessity to control the crystal density in addition to the alignment between crystals. Due to the similarity of useful anisotropic properties presented by some MOF crystals, 145  control over crystal alignment would represent a clear technological advantage.
## www.MaterialsViews.com
Another crucial aspect that needs to be considered is the accurate identification of the mechanical properties and durability which represent the key to the advancement of new technologies. 146  A significant achievement in this field would be the development of screening tests to measure the crystal adhesion with different substrates and the mechanical properties and durability of the positioned MOFs in varying service environments. These measurements are important for the evaluation of the service lifetime of MOF based devices. In fact, if the static location of the MOF is a crucial property, then the detachment/removal of the porous crystals from the substrate could represent device failure. In the case of a device which uses pressure gradients (e.g. microfluidics) the adhesion/anchorage is expected to be of critical concern. Depending on the application and MOF property, four methods can possibly be used to fix the porous crystals onto a surface: the use of functional groups producing a chemical bond between the substrate and the MOFs; the growth of MOFs on rough surfaces for physical anchorage of the MOF; the growth or positioning of crystals within confined spaces; the use of an adhesive to attach preformed MOFs.
Promising patterning techniques  Because of the potential applications, new patterning approaches are expected to impact on the ability to control the MOF position. Lithographic techniques previously explored for other materials can offer an important source of inspiration towards new concepts and protocols. For instance, controlled dewetting approaches have successfully been used for the spontaneous formation of patterned nanoparticles 147  from a colloidal solution (colloidal lithography). 96 Well defined hydrophobic/hydrophilic areas and the control of deposition parameters has offered an efficient solution for the positioning of self-assembled materials within selected regions, including the ordered arrangement of nanoparticles. 148
In the case of MOF patterning technology, the fabrication through colloidal solutions (MOFs or seeding agents) has initially proved to be an effective technique for film preparation or for the selected nucleation of ultra-porous crystals on a millimetre scale area. 91  Therefore, combining this approach by investigating the key deposition parameters, lateral resolution on the micron scale should be achievable. A possible limitation could again be the ability to promote strong adhesion between the substrate and the deposited materials.
Other inspiring techniques could possibly involve physical template-guided self-organization of colloids into 3D lithographed substrates, where patterns physically obstruct and guide the movement of nanoparticles/nanocrystals during dewetting/evaporation processes. 147  A preliminary study has been conducted confining MOF growth to within microfabricated wells showing much potential. 37  However in this case there are three concerns: the minimum size of the crystal that can be nucleated in the wells, the possible requirement to completely seal the lithographed cylindrical holes with MOFs and the need to have high aspect ratio patterns. The advantages are the potential to induce the formation of a geometrically controlled crystal shape and the ability to fix the crystals within microchannels due to the physical confinement.
Precise control over the position of preformed units (microparticles) has been successfully achieved by use of dry templatedirected self-assembly on a patterned metal electrode using an electrical field. 149  This process, involving electrostatic interactions, presents a promising approach for positioning uniform micron sized units and provides an efficient tool for further processing of these units. This process has not yet been applied to the manipulation of MOFs.
Another fascinating tool for positioning nano- and micromaterials is provided by optical tweezers. 150  Although this technique is not suitable for industrial device fabrication, it could offer the ability to test devices where the precise position of different MOFs or framework composites, (FCs) 151 35  featuring different properties within the same platform is needed.
Microfluidic systems offer the possibility to induce the growth of crystals in controlled locations as well. A successful system involves the controlled reaction between immiscible solvents as proposed by the De Vos group. 152  Control over the “fluid dynamics” allows for a reaction at the interface between miscible solvents carrying different reagents. Using this concept, Whitesides’s group 153  has successfully grown inorganic crystals in microfluidic channels using the laminar flow (FLO) fabrication method. This approach would possess some limitations related to versatility of positioning the ultra-porous crystals; however, it also offers the advantage of using the same microfluidic channel for MOF synthesis which is then used to exploit the MOF properties.
The same group 154  also showed in 2002 how it is possible to fabricate an ordered array of encapsulated inorganic and organic crystals. A lithographed substrate is withdrawn from a solution containing the crystal precursors. Crystal size and orientation can be controlled depending on the precursor concentration, the symmetry of the patterned wells and the withdrawal direction. Such a methodology could be suitable for the preparation of an ordered array of MOF crystals.
Synergy between MOF preparation and lithography  For MOF based device fabrication, it is expected that controlling the growth of MOFs with various functionalities will enable new capabilities in different fields such as electronics, optics, sensing, separation and controlled delivery. However, in this early stage of research, the majority of the proposed positioning processes for MOFs have been tested on metal organic framework archetypes (e.g. MOF-5 and HKUST-1). The synergy between the patterning research and the synthetic research is fundamental to address the requirement for MOF design with properties such as water/moisture resistance, biocompatibility, and particular optical, electronic and magnetic properties. In addition, mild synthetic conditions (low temperature, non-aggressive solvent) would enable the integration of MOFs in multifunctional integrated platforms, where delicate components could be used as well.
